          "page": null,
          "quote": "Redis AOF persists writes to an append-only file.",
          "section": "",
          "source": "four-container-probe.txt"
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "e1bd425d-ef08-5201-bd0b-e6de14504954",
          "citation_id": "E-e1bd425d-ef08-5201-bd0b-e6de14504954",
          "doc_id": "36a4bda0211e01bc9c521aac93b0fed98c2e095333a1bcf8c93a7ae87b7bbf38",
          "page": 1,
          "quote": "Redis AOF persistence records every write command.",
          "section": "",
          "source": "sample.pdf"
        }
      ],
      "conclusion": "The imported documents support only a minimal description of Redis AOF persistence: AOF records every write command and persists writes to an append-only file, which is contrasted with RDB's periodic snapshots. Two independent sources agree on this core point and no conflict was found. However, the corpus contains no detail on fsync/appendfsync policy, AOF rewrite behavior, or durability guarantees, and no throughput or benchmark data at all, so production throughput cannot be confirmed.",
      "disagreements": [],
      "findings": [
        {
          "citation_ids": [
            "E-e1bd425d-ef08-5201-bd0b-e6de14504954"
          ],
          "text": "AOF persistence is described as recording every write command, and is presented alongside RDB, which creates periodic snapshots for backups. This establishes the basic AOF-vs-RDB distinction (command logging vs. point-in-time snapshot)."
        },
        {
          "citation_ids": [
            "E-d00ec1e6-0304-5b00-89a6-272d0798ab8b"
          ],
          "text": "A second, independent document states that Redis AOF persists writes to an append-only file, corroborating the write-recording characterization from the other source."
        },
        {
          "citation_ids": [
            "E-e1bd425d-ef08-5201-bd0b-e6de14504954",
            "E-d00ec1e6-0304-5b00-89a6-272d0798ab8b"
          ],
          "text": "Comparison of the two AOF sources found agreement on the single point that AOF records writes (append-only file / every write command) and no conflicts; the comparison tool explicitly noted that the evidence does not cover fsync policy, durability guarantees, or the RDB/AOF relationship in depth."
        }
      ],
      "insufficient_evidence": false,
      "limitations": [
        "No evidence in the corpus describes appendfsync/fsync policy, so durability trade-offs (e.g., everysec vs. always) cannot be stated.",
        "No evidence describes AOF rewrite (BGREWRITEAOF), auto-rewrite thresholds, or AOF file growth/compaction behavior.",
        "No evidence describes AOF loading on restart, mixed RDB+AOF configurations, or the multi-part AOF manifest.",
        "Production throughput cannot be confirmed: searches for Redis throughput/benchmark operations-per-second returned no relevant evidence, and no performance figures appear in any retrieved document.",
        "The retrieved corpus is very small (two short statements about AOF plus unrelated probe/vector-store snippets), so the explanation is necessarily shallow and should not be treated as a complete AOF reference."
      ],
      "title": "Redis AOF Persistence: Evidence-Bounded Summary"
    },
